cartridge "LINCOLN GREEN/R.BIXBY/1988"

' LINCOLN GREEN
' A ColecoVision game based on "Lincoln Green" by Robert Bixby, COMPUTE!'s
' Gazette September 1988 (Commodore 64 machine language, SYS 2099; disk
' 1988-09). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026,
' in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/lincoln.dis, and watched in jsc64), the game is built
' for the ColecoVision: round oaks on a forest floor, a hooded outlaw in
' green as a two-colour sprite, arrows as smooth sprites, the forest
' scrolling a cell at a time, sound effects and a title tune.
'
' The game: an outlaw is lost in an endless forest with archers on his
' trail. Somewhere lies a sanctuary: the two numbers at the bottom are how
' far it is east-west and north-south, but not in which direction - walk
' and watch them. Bring both to 0. Arrows fly at you in volleys; nine nicks
' and you are caught. Walking into a tree costs money from your purse;
' an empty purse ends the game too.
'
' From the original (orig/): the forest of 4 x 4 cell blocks, each new
' block a tree or a clearing with even odds (SID noise bit); the outlaw
' stays in the middle and the forest scrolls four cells for a step of one
' block; only straight stick directions walk (diagonals do nothing); the
' two distances start at 1100, a step changes one of them by 10 and the
' sanctuary's direction on each axis is random; a distance that would go
' below 0 stays at 0 and its direction flips (so overshooting counts up
' again); both 0 = YOU REACHED SANCTUARY; pushing into a tree takes 1 from
' the purse of 600 for every pass of the C64's loop (about 33 a second,
' measured in jsc64; here 1 every 2 frames = 30 a second); an arrow hit is
' a nick, the 10th count (9 hits, the C64 counted from 1) ends the game;
' a volley picks one of 8 kinds at random with a hiss: four arrows from the
' left, top, top-left and bottom-left flying through the outlaw's spot
' (or the mirror image from the right and below), 1 pixel a frame, 2 when
' the two distances' high bytes add up to less than 4; which of the four
' arrows are real depends on that sum too (the C64's table at $1521: the
' closer you are, the more arrows); arrows stay put in the forest as it
' scrolls (8 pixels a cell).
'
' Liberties: a new block is a tree 3 times in 8 (the C64: 1 in 2, below
' the percolation threshold: the forest walled the outlaw in for good, as
' blocks that scroll away are forgotten); pushing into a tree for 48 frames
' (24 from the purse) forces a way through it, so no pocket is a trap
' (the C64 only took the money); the start glade is clear as on the C64,
' its hidden edge already chosen; the field is 7 x 5 blocks (the C64 had 10 x 6, its left four
' columns and top four rows drawn invisible); a volley ends when its
' arrows have flown 224 pixels (the C64 ended it when one chosen arrow
' left the screen); a hit ends the volley and the next comes 45 frames
' later (the C64 put the arrows back at their start); arrival times are
' staggered as on the C64 (104/88/80/64 pixels from the outlaw); the
' reverse volley's vertical arrow comes up the outlaw's column (the C64
' sent it up the far left edge, a slip); the sum for the arrow table stops
' at 10 (the C64 read past its table); hits are tested on the arrow's
' middle within 5 x 6 pixels of the outlaw's (the C64 used sprite
' collisions); distances stop at 9990; one step of the scroll every three
' frames (12 frames a block, the C64 took 12-17 jiffies); the outlaw turns
' red for a moment when nicked; sanctuary is spelt right (SANCUVERY on the
' C64); the end goes back to the title (the C64 asked PLAY AGAIN? Y/N).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick walks north, south, west, east.
' On the title, up and down choose START, HOW TO PLAY or CREDITS (the
' cursor back on START each time), a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

